-- #1111 A-2.7 SCOPE PROOF: the fix must be keyed PER MODULE, and this is the
-- program that observes a collision if it is not.
--
-- Two consumers with no import relationship, `usea` and `useb`, each write the bare
-- spelling `Pin`.  They must mean DIFFERENT declarations — sxa's arity-1 `Pin` and
-- sxb's arity-2 `Pin` — and each consumer reaches its own only through its own
-- `T(..)` member.  A binding built once for the whole program instead of once per
-- importing module can satisfy at most one of them, whichever registration wins,
-- so "it is keyed per module" is asserted by a running program here rather than by
-- a comment.
--
-- The third line is a genuine BYSTANDER: `bystand.mdk` imports nothing, declares no
-- colliding name, and only supplies a number.  It is here because a frame-keying
-- change is a program-global-table change, whose signature failure is that the
-- feature works and something unrelated breaks.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: `ra` = `unSA (Pin 7)` = `7`; `rb` = `unSB (Pin 2 3)` = `2 * 3` = `6`;
-- `bys` = `6 + 5` = `11`.  So `7`, `6`, `11`.
--
-- ⚠️ COUNTERFACTUAL, measured on a MEDAKA_STRICT=1 binary built from pristine
-- `main` `ae39ec41` (which IS "A-2.7 removed" — this branch has merged everything
-- else in `main`): RED.  Both `Pin` spellings fell through to the one bare-name
-- global cell, so exactly one consumer got the other's constructor.  Recorded in
-- the PR body with the exact engine output.
import usea.{ra}
import useb.{rb}
import bystand.{bys}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString ra)
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString rb)
  putStrLn (intToString bys)
